+## Networking notes
+
+- **IPv6 is link-local only, and only on the USB-C LAN (`usb0`).** It exists
+  solely for Reticulum's `AutoInterface`, which discovers peers over IPv6
+  link-local multicast (`ff12::`, UDP 29716/29717, data on 42671). Every other
+  interface — `eth0`, the WiFi client and hotspot, `sl0` — has IPv6 disabled
+  (`S09ipv6`); the box does no IPv6 routing, NAT, RA or DHCPv6, has no IPv6
+  internet, and dnsmasq filters AAAA records for LAN clients.
+- For AutoInterface discovery, the host's USB network adapter must have IPv6
+  enabled (the default on Windows, macOS and Linux). Otherwise point a
+  `TCPClientInterface` at `10.42.0.1:4242`. Hotspot clients always use TCP
+  (`192.168.42.1:4242`); AutoInterface does not run on the hotspot.
+- RNS adopts an AutoInterface interface only when `rnsd` starts, and `usb0`
+  gets its link-local address only once a USB host brings the link up. If the
+  host attaches after `rnsd` started (box powered separately, or a slow first
+  driver install), `S46wanwatch` restarts `rnsd` once when the address appears.
+- **Firewall fail-safe.** The ruleset is regenerated at boot and
+  pre-validated (`nft -c`) before it is swapped in. If generation fails on a
+  first boot, the shipped fallback `/etc/nftables.conf` is LAN-only: the
+  USB-C LAN keeps SSH, the web UI and NAT'd internet, while no WAN SSH, port
+  forwards or open ports are allowed. If even that can't load, a built-in
+  IPv4-only emergency ruleset is used; if nf_tables is unusable, forwarding
+  stays off. Details go to `/var/log/nftgen.err`. The same emergency ruleset
+  (USB-C LAN only) is loaded before the WAN gets an address at boot (`S29fw`)
+  and when the firewall is stopped at shutdown, so WAN SSH is never briefly
+  open while the box boots.
+- **eth0 is configured only when it is the WAN.** With `wlan0` as the WAN
+  (W board), `eth0` gets no DHCP client, address or route, so plugging a cable
+  in can't take over the default route.
+- **Logs.** `rnsd`, `lxmd` and `nomadnet` log to syslog (`/var/log/messages`,
+  tags `rnsd[pid]` etc.), which is RAM-backed and rotated. `/var/log` lives on
+  the 16 MB `/tmp` tmpfs.
+- **Per-unit identity.** The eth0 MAC, the USB serial number, the host-side
+  USB network MAC (which names a Linux host's `enx…` interface) and the box's
+  own USB network MAC (and so its `usb0` IPv6 link-local, which Reticulum's
+  AutoInterface uses to tell boxes on one host apart) are derived
+  per unit, from the chip UID where available, else from the microSD card's
+  CID. They stay the same across reboots and reflashes of the same card;
+  moving the card to another board moves the identity with it. Units upgraded
+  from an image that shared one MAC get a new DHCP lease once and appear once
+  as a new USB device.
 
